Internet of medical things: A systematic review
Authors: Chenxi Huang, Jian Wang, Shuihua Wang, Yudong Zhang
Journal: Neurocomputing
Year: 2023
Article: 126719
DOI: 10.1016/j.neucom.2023.126719
What the paper is about
This paper provides a systematic review of the Internet of Medical Things (IoMT) and its use in healthcare.
It examines how connected healthcare devices and sensing technologies can be used to collect, transmit and process medical information.
The review considers the broader IoMT ecosystem, including connected devices, communication, intelligent processing and healthcare applications.
Main concepts
IoMT connects healthcare devices.
Sensors and medical devices can act as connected nodes rather than isolated devices.
Data moves through multiple layers.
A healthcare IoT system commonly includes sensing, communication, processing and application layers.
Healthcare IoT has challenges.
Important challenges include:
Security
Privacy
Reliability
Interoperability
Scalability
Data management
CAREGRID connection
CAREGRID applies the IoMT concept through:
Sensor Layer
↓
Gateway / Communication
↓
Processing
↓
Hospital Dashboard
The individual CAREGRID sensors therefore form part of a wider IoMT-style architecture.
Design decision influenced
Keep CAREGRID modular, so individual sensor nodes can be expanded without redesigning the complete system.
What this paper does NOT prove
It does not prove that CAREGRID's current prototype is clinically effective. It provides background and architecture guidance.